I'm trusting God because He's promised something. And when He promises, He provides all that is necessary for me to accomplish His purpose. So if I'm doing things that don't align with His will or His plan, then I’m not fulfilling His purpose. That means I’m not receiving what He promised. So I have to be careful about what I’m doing. I have to stay aligned with Him.

We can see this in Scripture where Paul says: “For I know the plans I have for you,” declares the Lord, “plans to prosper you and not to harm you, plans to give you hope and a future.” — Jeremiah 29:11

So as long as I’m walking with Him, staying close to Him, being obedient, I’m living out His promise. Because He doesn’t leave me alone; He walks with me. He guides me. He gives me wisdom. He equips me. He empowers me. All these things are part of His promise. So even though there may be challenges along the way—obstacles, trials—I know that He’s with me every step of the way.

And so, when I look at what I’m doing now, I ask myself: Is this aligned with His will? Am I moving forward in faith, not fear? Am I relying on His strength rather than my own?

If I’m not sure whether something is from God, I’ll pause and pray. I’ll seek His guidance through prayer and Scripture. I’ll listen to wise counsel. And I’ll wait until I feel peace in my heart before I move forward.

But here’s the thing: I don’t believe in waiting passively. I don’t think God wants us to sit around doing nothing while we wait for Him to act. He calls us to action. He calls us to walk in obedience. He calls us to serve others. He calls us to share our faith. He calls us to live boldly.

So yes, I trust God. But I also take steps of faith. I don’t wait for some dramatic sign. I respond to the small nudges—the quiet promptings of the Holy Spirit. I follow those. I obey them. And I watch how God moves.

The bottom line is this: Trusting God isn’t passive. It’s active. It’s bold. It’s faithful. It’s ongoing. And it requires discipline and intentionality.

Now, let’s talk about the power of belief. When you truly believe that God is who He says He is—and that He will do what He promises—you begin to experience the reality of His presence and provision. Belief transforms your actions. It changes your perspective. It shapes your decisions.

Because if you believe God is good, you won’t fear. If you believe God is sovereign, you won’t panic. If you believe God is trustworthy, you won’t doubt. And if you believe God is present, you won’t despair.

That’s why belief matters. Not just intellectual agreement—but deep, lived conviction. The kind of belief that makes you get up in the morning and say, “Today, I’m stepping into what God has prepared for me.”

So, if you’re struggling right now—if you’re uncertain, anxious, overwhelmed—remember this: God hasn’t forgotten you. He’s still working. He’s still guiding. He’s still providing. And He’s still calling you to step forward in faith.

Trust Him. Take that next step. Even if it feels scary. Especially if it feels scary. Because that’s often where God meets you.

